WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.402
One Postgres node stretches a long way.

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:08.803
But it cannot do two opposite jobs at once.

00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:12.205
Take a million writes a second, and scan fifty billion rows for a dashboard.

00:00:12.205 --> 00:00:15.607
At big tech, the data stack splits in three.

00:00:15.607 --> 00:00:19.415
Start with the transactional side.

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:23.223
OLTP.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:27.031
Every play, like, and follow is one fast write, under a hundred milliseconds, on Postgres or Spanner.

00:00:27.031 --> 00:00:30.840
Then a pipeline carries the data across and reshapes it, about a hundred terabytes a day.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.648
It lands on the analytics side.

00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:38.456
OLAP.

00:00:38.456 --> 00:00:42.264
Here one query scans billions of rows on BigQuery to answer what is trending.

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:46.072
Opposite jobs, opposite engines.

00:00:46.072 --> 00:00:48.914
Why not run the dashboard on the live database?

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:51.756
Not locks.

00:00:51.756 --> 00:00:54.599
MVCC avoids those.

00:00:54.599 --> 00:00:57.441
The cost is contention.

00:00:57.441 --> 00:01:00.283
One long scan hogs the disk, evicts the hot cash, and stalls cleanup until the database bloats.

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:03.619
So analytics gets its own engine, and it is always distributed.

00:01:03.619 --> 00:01:06.955
One machine scanning a terabyte takes two hundred seconds.

00:01:06.955 --> 00:01:10.290
A hundred machines, each scanning ten gigabytes?

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.626
Two seconds.

00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:16.962
Same work, split across the cluster, a hundred times faster.

00:01:16.962 --> 00:01:21.444
How does the data cross the bridge?

00:01:21.444 --> 00:01:25.925
The old way pulls a giant nightly query from the live database, the exact scan we just avoided.

00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:30.406
Change data capture flips it.

00:01:30.406 --> 00:01:34.888
Tail the write-ahead log the database already writes, and every committed change becomes an event in seconds, shipped straight onto Kafka.

00:01:34.888 --> 00:01:37.665
So the stack is three systems.

00:01:37.665 --> 00:01:40.442
Transactions on one, analytics on another, a pipeline between.

00:01:40.442 --> 00:01:43.220
All of it SQL underneath.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.997
Next question.

00:01:45.997 --> 00:01:48.774
When is SQL itself the wrong tool?
